## Diagnosis

Both failing tests live in one file, `tests/suite/calc_test.go` (package `suite_test`), and both assert on the `fixture/buildcodefix/services/calc` package:

- `TestA` calls `calc.A()` and fails with `t.Fatal("SHARED-FAILURE-EVIDENCE")` unless the return value is `1`.
- `TestB` calls `calc.B()` and fails with the same message unless the return value is `2`.

The only production file in the declared write root is `services/calc/calc.go`:

```
package calc
func A() int{return 0}
func B() int{return 0}
```

Both exported functions are stubs returning `0`, so both assertions fail. The shared `SHARED-FAILURE-EVIDENCE` string is why the harness grouped the two tests into a single failure group — it is not evidence of a single shared root cause beyond "the `calc` package is unimplemented". There is one defect class (stubbed return values) and two concrete contract violations, one per test.

There is no missing dependency, no build failure, and no wiring problem: `go.mod` declares module `fixture/buildcodefix`, the test imports `fixture/buildcodefix/services/calc`, and that path resolves to the existing `services/calc` package. Compilation already succeeds (the failures are assertion failures at `calc_test.go:3`, not a load error). So the repair is purely about return values, not about package layout, module paths, or new files.

